<section class="phero"><div class="phero-media"><img src="/assets/img/photos/review.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/review-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/review.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="900" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.1"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li><a href="/resources/">Resources</a></li><li><a href="/resources/guides/">Guides</a></li><li aria-current="page">Reading an insurance estimate</li></ol></nav><p class="kicker kicker-l">Guide · 7 min read · June 30, 2026</p><h1>How to Read an Insurance Estimate Line by Line</h1><p class="lede">Insurance estimates are dense, but they follow a pattern. Learn where to look and which omissions cost owners the most.</p></div><svg class="ridge" viewBox="0 0 1440 90" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M0 90V62l110-30 60 22 130-50 90 44 60-16 120 38 150-62 80 34 90-20 130 44 110-34 70 18 140-48 100 40v48z"/></svg></section>
<div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose">
<p>Most carrier estimates are produced in estimating software and follow a similar layout. Once you know where to look, you can spot the gaps that matter.</p>
<h2>The summary page</h2>
<p>Start at the end. The summary shows replacement cost value (RCV), depreciation, actual cash value (ACV), the deductible and the net claim. Check that the deductible matches your declarations page — especially if you have a percentage wind/hail deductible.</p>
<h2>Areas and quantities</h2>
<p>The estimate is organized by area: roof, elevations, rooms. Each area lists measurements. Compare roof squares, wall areas and linear feet with your own measurements or an aerial report. Small measurement errors multiply across every line in that area.</p>
<h2>Line items</h2>
<p>Each line has a description, quantity, unit price and total. Watch for:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Repair versus replace</strong> — “R&amp;R” means remove and replace; “repair” or “detach and reset” mean something cheaper.</li>
<li><strong>Missing tear-off</strong> or disposal.</li>
<li><strong>Underlayment, ice barrier, drip edge and starter</strong> on roofs.</li>
<li><strong>Paint, texture and trim</strong> after drywall repairs.</li>
<li><strong>Steep and high charges</strong> on roofs.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Depreciation</h2>
<p>Depreciation is usually shown per line or per area. Check the age and life used, and whether non-material items like labor are depreciated. Our <a href="/claim-planning-tools/rcv-acv-holdback-calculator/">RCV / ACV holdback calculator</a> helps model the numbers.</p>
<h2>Overhead and profit</h2>
<p>If your repair needs several trades coordinated by a general contractor, look for overhead and profit. Its absence on a multi-trade job is one of the most common gaps.</p>
<h2>What to do with what you find</h2>
<p>List the differences, attach photos and your contractor’s estimate, and send a supplement request. If the gap is large and the carrier won’t move, a professional <a href="/colorado-claim-appraisal/carrier-estimate-review/">carrier estimate review</a> is the logical next step before appraisal.</p>
